{"task_id": "Mbpp/305", "solution": "def start_withp(input_list: list) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts the first two words starting with 'p' (case-sensitive) from a list of strings.\n    Each string in the input list is split by spaces to find individual words.\n    \"\"\"\n    p_words = []\n    for s in input_list:\n        words = s.split(' ')\n        for word in words:\n            if word and word[0] == 'P':\n                p_words.append(word)\n                if len(p_words) == 2:\n                    return (p_words[0], p_words[1])\n    return (p_words[0], p_words[1]) # This line assumes there will always be at least two 'P' words as per the problem description's implicit contract."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/301", "solution": "```python\ndef dict_depth(dictionary: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the maximum nesting depth of a dictionary.\n    This function processes mixed data types, ignoring non-dictionary values for depth calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not dictionary:\n        return 1\n\n    max_depth = 1\n    \n    # Internal helper function for recursive depth calculation.\n    def _calculate_depth(obj: any, current_depth: int) -> int:\n        nonlocal max_depth\n        if isinstance(obj, dict):\n            max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            for value in obj.values():\n                _calculate_depth(value, current_depth + 1)\n        return max_depth\n\n    for value in dictionary.values():\n        if isinstance(value, dict):\n            _calculate_depth(value, 2) # Start inner dictionaries at depth 2\n        \n    return max_depth\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/300", "solution": "def count_binary_seq(n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts binary sequences of length 2n where the sum of the first n bits equals the sum of the last n bits.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class BinarySequence:\n        \"\"\"Represents a binary sequence and calculates sums of its halves.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, sequence_str: str, n_val: int):\n            self.sequence = sequence_str\n            self.n = n_val\n            self.first_half_sum = 0\n            self.second_half_sum = 0\n            \n            for i in range(self.n):\n                self.first_half_sum += int(self.sequence[i])\n            \n            for i in range(self.n, 2 * self.n):\n                self.second_half_sum += int(self.sequence[i])\n\n        def is_valid_sequence(self) -> bool:\n            \"\"\"Checks if the sum of the first half equals the sum of the second half.\"\"\"\n            return self.first_half_sum == self.second_half_sum\n\n    class SequenceCounter:\n        \"\"\"Generates and counts valid binary sequences based on the given criteria.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, n_val: int):\n            self.n = n_val\n            self.valid_sequences = 0\n\n        def count(self) -> float:\n            \"\"\"Iterates through all possible binary sequences and counts valid ones.\"\"\"\n            total_length = 2 * self.n\n            \n            for i in range(1 << total_length):\n                # Convert integer i to a binary string, padded with leading zeros\n                binary_str = bin(i)[2:].zfill(total_length)\n                \n                seq_obj = BinarySequence(binary_str, self.n)\n                if seq_obj.is_valid_sequence():\n                    self.valid_sequences += 1\n            \n            return float(self.valid_sequences)\n\n    # Main logic for the count_binary_seq function\n    counter = SequenceCounter(n)\n    return counter.count()"}
