{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "```python\ndef set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of n.\n    If all bits are set, it adds a new bit at the leftmost position.\"\"\"\n    \n    # Handle the case where n is 0. The leftmost unset bit is at position 0.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Find the position of the leftmost unset bit\n    # Iterate from left (most significant bit) to right\n    # Python's bit_length() gives the number of bits required to represent n, excluding leading zeros.\n    # So, the highest bit position is bit_length - 1.\n    \n    # We want to find the leftmost 0.\n    # Start checking from the most significant bit that *could* be set.\n    # The maximum possible bit length for an integer is not fixed, but we can assume\n    # we're looking within the current effective length of `n` or one bit beyond.\n    \n    # A common approach for \"leftmost unset\" (especially in fixed-width systems)\n    # is to check from MSB downwards. For Python's arbitrary precision integers,\n    # the \"leftmost\" usually refers to the most significant bit position.\n    \n    # Let's find the position of the leftmost unset bit.\n    # We can iterate from the current bit_length downwards.\n    \n    current_bit_length = n.bit_length()\n    \n    # Check from bit_length - 1 down to 0\n    for i in range(current_bit_length - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the bit at position 'i' is unset (0)\n        if not (n & (1 << i)):\n            # If it's unset, set this bit and return\n            return n | (1 << i)\n            \n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits up to current_bit_length - 1 are set.\n    # This implies the number is of the form 2^k - 1 (e.g., 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, ...).\n    # In this case, the leftmost unset bit is effectively at position current_bit_length,\n    # which is equivalent to 2^current_bit_length.\n    return n | (1 << current_bit_length)\n\n```"}
